\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces  \textbf  {Shallow shift-invariant alternative to the QFT.} (a) Shift invariance is a critical property of the QFT. We identify a circuit family, denoted as \text  {HP}-k, that inherently preserves the property. Within this broader family, \text  {HP}-1 specifically serves as shallow-depth gates that are friendly to experiment. (b) In the proposed HSP algorithmic routine, the deep QFT block (the left gray gate) is directly replaced by the \text  {HP}circuit to extract subgroup information prior to measurement. A neural network then decodes the measurement outcomes, bypassing the standard continued fraction expansion (the right gray gate) typically used in classical post-processing.}}{1}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:illus}{{1}{1}{\textbf {Shallow shift-invariant alternative to the QFT.} (a) Shift invariance is a critical property of the QFT. We identify a circuit family, denoted as \myqc -k, that inherently preserves the property. Within this broader family, \myqc -1 specifically serves as shallow-depth gates that are friendly to experiment. (b) In the proposed HSP algorithmic routine, the deep QFT block (the left gray gate) is directly replaced by the \myqc circuit to extract subgroup information prior to measurement. A neural network then decodes the measurement outcomes, bypassing the standard continued fraction expansion (the right gray gate) typically used in classical post-processing}{figure.1}{}}
