\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces  Numerical Fisher-information benchmarks for the candidate circuit families. (a) Minimum discrete Fisher information as a function of the number of qubits for the QFT reference and the HP-family circuits. The HP-family curves remain below the QFT benchmark, but all increase with system size. (b) Fisher-information distributions as a function of the number of layers, showing how additional layers increase the retained information. Appendix~\ref {app:fi_log_linear_growth} shows that, over the sampled qubit window, both the QFT reference and the HP-family curves are well described by the same log-linear model $\ln I_{\min }(n)=kn+b$, corresponding to exponential growth in $n$, with smaller effective exponents for the HP-family circuits.}}{2}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:fi_scaling}{{2}{2}{Numerical Fisher-information benchmarks for the candidate circuit families. (a) Minimum discrete Fisher information as a function of the number of qubits for the QFT reference and the HP-family circuits. The HP-family curves remain below the QFT benchmark, but all increase with system size. (b) Fisher-information distributions as a function of the number of layers, showing how additional layers increase the retained information. Appendix~\ref {app:fi_log_linear_growth} shows that, over the sampled qubit window, both the QFT reference and the HP-family curves are well described by the same log-linear model $\ln I_{\min }(n)=kn+b$, corresponding to exponential growth in $n$, with smaller effective exponents for the HP-family circuits}{figure.2}{}}
\newlabel{thm:necessary_modulus_condition}{{1}{3}{Necessary condition of shift invariance}{theorem.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces  Classical post-processing pipeline for subgroup recovery from \text  {HP}-1 measurement data. The measured bit strings produced after the shallow quantum circuit are passed through a shared feature map and then ranked by a beam-search decoder to infer the candidate period. The figure illustrates that the measurement outcomes still retain period-dependent structure after the HP transformation, which can be extracted by an efficient neural post-processing stage rather than by an explicit QFT inversion.}}{4}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:recovery_pipeline}{{3}{4}{Classical post-processing pipeline for subgroup recovery from \myqc -1 measurement data. The measured bit strings produced after the shallow quantum circuit are passed through a shared feature map and then ranked by a beam-search decoder to infer the candidate period. The figure illustrates that the measurement outcomes still retain period-dependent structure after the HP transformation, which can be extracted by an efficient neural post-processing stage rather than by an explicit QFT inversion}{figure.3}{}}
